.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines test a property of a program on many inputs, and when one fails,
make it as small as they can before they show it.
A property is a function,
.IR Prop ,
that makes an input and checks the program on it.
It returns nil when the property holds, and otherwise a string that says what went wrong,
which the library does not free.
.PP
A property makes its input from the choices that a source,
.IR s ,
gives it.
A choice is a number from 0 to a bound, and 0 is the simplest.
A source gives random choices, or replays the choices a test gives it, and records each choice.
To shrink a failing input, the library changes the recorded choices, never the values made from them,
and runs the property again.
So the library can make at random, replay and shrink any input that these routines make,
or that ordinary C makes on top of them.
The simpler choice should make the simpler input: the first of the alternatives, the shorter list,
the smaller number.
.SS Choices
.I Choose
gives a choice from 0 to
.IR n \-1;
an
.I n
of 0 allows any
.BR uvlong .
At random, it gives 0,
.IR n \-1
and small numbers more often than others.
.I Coin
gives 1 with a chance of
.I pct
percent, and 0 otherwise.
.I Pick
gives an index from 0 to
.IR nw \-1,
at random by the weights
.IR w ,
or evenly if
.I w
is nil.
.PP
.I More
says whether a sequence gets one more element.
When it gives 1, it begins a span that holds its own choice, and the caller ends the span with
.I endspan
once it has made the element:
.IP
.EX
while(more(s)){
	v[n++] = choose(s, 100);
	endspan(s);
}
.EE
.PP
At random, sequences get longer over the runs of
.IR forall .
.I Span
and
.I endspan
mark the choices that make one part of an input, such as an operation and its arguments,
so the shrinker can delete the part whole or swap it with the part beside it.
Spans nest.
When the property returns, the library ends any span left open.
.PP
.I Discard
says the input does not apply.
It ends the run at once, with a
.I longjmp
(see
.IR setjmp (2));
.I forall
does not count the run, and makes another input.
Memory the property allocated is not freed.
.I Note
formats a line, as
.IR print (2)
does, and keeps it with the run; a failing case shows its notes.
.SS Running
.I Forall
runs the property
.I p
with its argument
.I a
on 100 random inputs.
If one fails, it shrinks the input, prints the property's name and string, its notes,
and a command that replays the case, and returns \-1.
If all pass, it prints nothing and returns 0.
It also fails if it discards more than ten times the number of runs, plus 100.
.PP
.I Propargs
takes a test program's arguments.
Each other argument names a property to run; with none,
.I forall
runs every property.
The options are:
.TP
.BI -n " runs"
Run each property on
.I runs
random inputs.
.TP
.BI -s " seed"
Start the random choices from
.IR seed .
A run with the same seed and properties gives the same inputs.
By default the seed is random, and a failure shows it.
.TP
.BI -r " choices"
Replay
.IR choices ,
numbers separated by blanks, once, and do not shrink.
If the argument holds anything else, it names a file that holds the numbers.
.TP
.BI -b " file"
Take the choices from the bytes of
.IR file ,
as
.I srcbytes
does, and shrink the input if it fails.
.TP
.B -v
Print a line for each property that passes.
.PD
.PP
.I Propexits
exits with the status
.B fail
if a property failed, and with an empty status otherwise.
.PP
A failure reads:
.IP
.EX
sortdup: sort lost an element
	in: 0 0
	out: 0
	found by run 2 with -s 1; 4 shrinks in 49 runs
	tprop -r '1 0 1 0 0' sortdup
.EE
.PP
The last line is the shrunk input's choices, as the property read them.
On a replay, choices past the end are 0, and a choice that is not below its bound is 0.
.SS Shrinking
The shrinker keeps a change only if the property still fails and the new choices are simpler:
fewer, or as many and smaller at the first place they differ.
It tries these changes in order, and starts again until none applies or it has run the
property 20000 times:
deleting each span;
deleting each run of 8, 4, 2 and then 1 choices;
putting in place of a span of one of
.IR expand 's
symbols a span of the same symbol inside it;
lowering each choice, to 0 or else by halves;
lowering together the choices that are equal;
swapping neighboring choices that are out of order, and neighboring spans;
and moving an amount from a choice to one of the next four.
.SS Inputs
.I Expand
makes text from the grammar
.IR g ,
starting at the symbol
.IR start ,
and returns it in memory from
.IR malloc (2).
.I G
is a nil-terminated array of rules.
A rule is a symbol, a tab, and one alternative; a symbol's alternatives are its rules,
in order, and
.I expand
picks a rule given twice twice as often.
In an alternative,
.BI < sym >
is the symbol
.IR sym ,
and
.BI [ set ]
is one rune from
.IR set ,
which may hold ranges such as
.BR a-z .
After either,
.B *
gives any number of them,
.B +
for one or more, or
.B ?
for one or none.
A backslash quotes the next character, and any other character stands for itself.
Each symbol expanded is a span, and each element of a repetition is a span of its own.
Below
.I depth
symbols, a symbol takes its first alternative and repeats no symbol,
so the first alternative of each symbol must not lead back to it.
.PP
.I Mutate
makes text from the examples
.IR c ,
an array of
.I nc
strings.
It picks an example, takes a window of it, and then makes any number of edits:
cutting a range, putting in a character, copying a piece of an example in, or copying a piece of
the text itself.
It returns the text in memory from
.IR malloc (2).
At random the window is mostly the whole example; it shrinks to the shortest window
of the first example that still fails, with the fewest edits.
The edits work on bytes, and put in only tabs, newlines, and printable ASCII.
.SS Other sources
.I Srcbytes
makes a source that takes its choices from the
.I n
bytes at
.IR b ,
as a coverage-guided fuzzer gives them.
A choice with the bound
.I n
takes as few bytes as hold
.IR n \-1,
high byte first, modulo
.IR n ;
past the end, the bytes are 0.
.I Runprop
runs
.I p
once on the choices of
.IR s ,
from the start, and returns the property's string if it fails, or nil if it passes or discards.
.I Srcfree
frees a source.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a sort on lists of numbers; the notes show the case.
.IP
.EX
char*
sorts(Src *s, void*)
{
	uvlong v[1000];
	int i, n;

	for(n = 0; n < nelem(v) && more(s); n++){
		v[n] = choose(s, 100);
		endspan(s);
	}
	mysort(v, n);
	for(i = 1; i < n; i++)
		if(v[i-1] > v[i])
			return "out of order";
	return nil;
}

void
main(int argc, char **argv)
{
	propargs(argc, argv);
	forall("sorts", sorts, nil);
	propexits();
}
.EE
.PP
Compare a program with a model: draw a sequence of operations, each a span with its arguments,
apply each to the queue and to an array, and compare them after each.
The shrinker deletes the operations that do not matter.
.IP
.EX
while(more(s)){
	switch(pick(s, nil, 2)){
	case 0:
		x = choose(s, 10);
		note(s, "put %llud", x);
		qput(q, x);
		m[n++] = x;
		break;
	case 1:
		note(s, "get");
		if(n > 0 && qget(q) != m[h++])
			return "get gave the wrong value";
		break;
	}
	endspan(s);
	if(qlen(q) != n-h)
		return "wrong length";
}
.EE
.PP
A round trip prints a value made from choices and parses it back:
.IP
.EX
v = mkval(s);
t = smprint("%V", v);
note(s, "%s", t);
if(!eqval(parseval(t), v))
	return "parse of print differs";
.EE
.PP
A differential test gives the same input to two programs that should agree, such as
an incremental parse and a whole one.
Make expressions from a grammar and compare two evaluators:
.IP
.EX
char *g[] = {
	"e	<n>",
	"e	(<e>)",
	"e	<e>+<e>",
	"e	<e>*<e>",
	"n	[0-9]+",
	nil
};

t = expand(s, g, "e", 8);
note(s, "%s", t);
if(eval1(t) != eval2(t))
	return "the evaluators differ";
.EE
.PP
Drive a property from a fuzzer:
.IP
.EX
int
LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(uchar *b, long n)
{
	Src *s;

	s = srcbytes(b, n);
	if(runprop(s, sorts, nil) != nil)
		abort();
	srcfree(s);
	return 0;
}
.EE
.PP
and shrink what it finds with
.BI -b " file" \fR.
